A||N||13||bHooper||Rear strut installation||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||10-24-2000||09:20 PM||||I bought a set of Gabrial struts to put on the my GT, started working on it tonight, and have a few questions as I go.<P>1) What is the easiest way to get the strut aligned up to the spindle, so I can put the bolts back in?  It seems to be "extended".<BR>2)If I change the struts now, do I have to get the car aligned b4 driving it?  I am going to be installing poly eurethane bushings in the next couple of weeks and wanted to hold off on alignment until this was done.<P>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||ka4nkf||10-24-2000||09:57 PM||||I do not know about Gabrial, but the monroe struts have a flange on them. you buy the strut camber bolts and It makes It easy to line up. the fiero store sells them. I put a level on the rim and set It to where the bubble is In the center. this will be very close until you can put on the machine. also the line up shop will probaly charge less If you have the new adjusting bolts because It make It a lot Easier.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PJ||10-24-2000||10:00 PM||||#1. I use a big pry bar.<P>#2. With new struts push in on the strut and knuckle to get as much neg. camber as you can and the alignment should be OK 'til you do the bushings.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR><p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||MCordray||10-24-2000||10:11 PM||||I just put some rear struts on my GT, about a month ago.  For me, I used a small hydraulic jack so that I could work with getting the spindle bolts back in. I replaced my struts with a pair on KYB.  About a year prior to that, I put in a pair of Gabriels, but the right one began leaking after only about six months.  I got them from Autozone, so if they're under warranty, you might want to save the receipt. I'm not saying you got bad struts, just an FYI.<P>Also, you will have to get a four wheel alignmnet after they're installed.  Personally, if you've got decent tires on now, I wouldn't wait over two weeks to get an alignment (depeneding on the amount of driving you do).  Just because it will be way out of wack when you get it back together.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||10-24-2000||10:36 PM||||Small changes in rear camber produce big changes in rear toe.  If the camber is not correctly adjusted, your car may handle very poorly. I witnessed this firsthand when I changes my rear struts about 2 weeks ago.  I would strongly suggest you do the bushings and struts at the same time, then minimize driving it until you get a 4-wheel alignment.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||10-24-2000||10:37 PM||||Try dropping your control arm down as far as it will go. Then it's either prybar or jack time to finish lining up the bolt holes. Those adjustable cam bolts are a good investment.<P>You will toast your rear tires if you try to drive it much. I tried using a level and everything I could thing of to get the alignment close after a strut change. It was still way out of alignment when I had the shop check it. I would do the bushings and struts at the same time and then immediately get it aligned.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||10-24-2000||11:35 PM||||When I installed struts on my '85 SE, I  scribed the suspension upright around the strut flange before removing the old struts.  When re-installing, I think I hung the new strut from the upper mount by one or two loose nuts, then aligned the lower bolts by wiggling and shoving.  After partly tightening the strut bolts, I pried the upright inward until the scribe marks aligned around the new strut.  Probably not super-precise, but it's working so far.<P>If you don't already have them, you might replace the strut cushions (boots), too.  Autozone's catalog doesn't list them for Fiero, but you can look them up in Gabriel's online catalog, and Autozone may have them in stock.<P>I agree that Gabriels may not be as good as KYB's, but they're half the price and way better than my worn-out stock struts.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||10-24-2000||11:42 PM||||The Gabriel Red Ryders are a good middle of the road budget gas strut, very similar to the Monroe struts in my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||10-25-2000||07:02 AM||||As far as the strut goes.. cheaper, generally means stifer... and I prefer stiffer suspensions.<P>The rear tires are a cheap pair of used Generals that I paid $50 for last week.  The fronts are a 6 y/o pair of Toyo's that are dry rotting.  I don't care what happens to the tires.<P>What dot he cam bolts look like, and where can I get a set of adjustable ones?<P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||10-25-2000||07:18 AM||||The bolt looks the same, but it has this concentric shaped washer like affair (the cam) that allows you to easily adjust the alignment. It goes in the lowest, slotted strut bolt hole. You can order them at large car part chain stores like Advance, O'Reilly, and NAPA. carparts.com has them too I think, and probably the Fiero Store.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||10-25-2000||07:05 PM||||i priced a adjustable cam bolt, at NAPA, today.  they ain't cheap... $18 and some change each!<P>is there a cheaper source?<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||ka4nkf||10-25-2000||09:39 PM||||The fiero store has the strut mounting kit<BR>(lower strut mounting bolts) Part # 57516<BR>Price $15.55. like I said before It makes It a lot easier.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mark||10-25-2000||10:16 PM||||Yeah, the cam bolts make camber adjustment easier, but I wasn't too interested in spending an extra $36 for essentially two cam-shaped washers tacked onto bolts that I already had.  Some alignment shops will insist on installing the cam bolts if not already installed (at an additional cost, of course), but the place I used (Monroe muffler) didn't say a word about it.  I just hope they did the adjustment right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||10-26-2000||09:14 AM||||Car Parts has the rear strut Cam bolt kit (part # K6264) for $14.97.  <P>Does it only require one cam bolt per side, or 2?  Do I need to replace the upper or lower of the two strut bolts?<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
